<.shell current={:top_bar}>
  <h1>Top bar</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Navy, with the amber rule. The team you're on leads it: the name opens the team picker, and your initials open your account menu.
  </p>

  <div class="demo bar-demo" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar
      current_user={sample_user()}
      current_team={sample_team()}
      current_member={sample_member()}
      team_admin?={true}
      managed_teams={sample_managed_teams()}
      member_logins={sample_member_logins()}
      current_path="/teams/squamish/activities"
    >
      <:search>
        <.search_box
          kinds={Web.Search.kinds(sample_team(), true, false)}
          team={sample_team()}
          id="search-demo-bar"
        />
      </:search>
    </Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar>
  </div>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every page is in one area: a team's space, an organization's, SAR Duty admin, or Account settings. The bar reads logo, a faint slash, then the area's name, such as "SAR Duty / Squamish SAR". There is one space per team: your role there decides which pages you see.
    </li>
    <li>
      The team picker is on the name for everyone logged in, even on one team. It lists your teams, each team where you are a team admin or a member, A to Z, under "Your team" or "Your teams". Then SAR Duty's own rows, in a navy band at the foot of the menu, so the wordmark keeps its colours: SAR Duty's home page, where Privacy and Terms are, and for SAR Duty admins, SAR Duty admin with a shield and the Style guide. On a phone's navy panel the band is a lighter strip. The team you're on is marked; a team opened from Admin › Teams that isn't yours marks nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      The logo always goes to the home page, and so does the picker's home row, so the picker is the same menu at every width. Logged in on the home, terms, or privacy page, the bar still names your team with the picker on it, marking nothing, so the public site never strands you.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a team's pages, <a href={page_path(:search)}>search</a>
      sits before your initials: a field on a desktop, a magnifying glass below 1024px.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your initials, top right, open your name and email, Account settings, and Log out. This menu is the same on every team: it is about your login, not the team.
    </li>
    <li>
      The area's own pages are in the <a href={page_path(:side_nav)}>side navigation</a>, not the bar, so the bar has room as teams get more pages.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>On a phone</h3>
  <p>
    Below 1024px the Menu button moves to the left, the area's name alone sits in the middle, and your initials stay on the right, so a phone or a shared iPad always shows who is logged in. The logo leaves the bar: people know which app they opened, and the picker's home row is one tap away. The name ends in an ellipsis when it is too long. The picker stays on the name, with a chevron, with the same rows as on a desktop. Menu holds the area's pages in their sections; with none, there is no Menu. Your initials open the same account menu as on a desktop. Each panel opens under the amber rule, the width of the screen, with the current page marked in amber. Opening one closes the other, and going to another page closes both.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href={page_path(:layout) <> "#narrow-pages"}>Narrow pages</a>
    use this bar at every width, lined up with their column. In the app:
    <code>site-bar-compact</code>
    on the bar or around it.
  </p>
  <div class="demo phone-width phone-bar-demo site-bar-compact" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar
      current_user={sample_user()}
      current_team={sample_team()}
      current_member={sample_member()}
      team_admin?={true}
      managed_teams={sample_managed_teams()}
      member_logins={sample_member_logins()}
      current_path="/teams/squamish/activities"
    >
      <:search>
        <.search_box
          kinds={Web.Search.kinds(sample_team(), true, false)}
          team={sample_team()}
          id="search-demo-phone"
        />
      </:search>
    </Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar>
  </div>

  <h3>Who sees which rows</h3>
  <p>
    Everyone logged in gets the picker. Each case on a desktop, then on a phone, with the picker open.
  </p>
  <%= for {id, title, bar} <- sample_switchers() do %>
    <h4>{title}</h4>
    <div id={"switcher-#{id}"} class="demo bar-demo switcher-demo" style="padding: 0">
      <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar {bar} />
    </div>
    <div
      id={"phone-switcher-#{id}"}
      class="demo phone-width phone-bar-demo site-bar-compact switcher-demo"
      style="padding: 0"
    >
      <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar {bar} />
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <script>
    // Open the menus, so the examples show them. Their shared name would let only one
    // stay open, so drop it here.
    document.querySelectorAll(
      ".bar-demo .team-switcher, .phone-bar-demo:not(.switcher-demo) .site-bar-phone, .switcher-demo .phone-team-switcher"
    ).forEach(function (menu) { menu.removeAttribute("name"); menu.open = true; });
  </script>

  <h3>Logged out</h3>
  <p>
    Log in is a link in the bar, white like the Menu button, at every width. It is not a
    button: it goes to a page.
  </p>
  <div class="demo" style="padding: 0">
    <Web.Layouts.main_nav_bar />
  </div>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    The layout's <code>main_nav_bar</code>, built from <code>&lt;.site_bar&gt;</code>.
    <code>Web.Navigation</code>
    decides the area, its pages, the switcher's rows, and the initials. The menus are open here so you can see them.
  </p>
</.shell>
